    topics: [{ workplace: "market" }, { goal: "sell-goods" }, { goal: "barter" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Kurmi market and the Kano caravans",
    text: "Kano's Kurmi market, said to date from the fifteenth century, drew caravans from across the Sahel and the Sahara. The German traveller Heinrich Barth described it in 1851 filled with cloth, salt, kola nuts from the Asante forests, natron, horses, slaves and European goods. Prices were in cowries, counted in strings of a hundred and bags of twenty thousand. Brokers, dillali, matched strangers with sellers and took a commission, and landlords, maigida, lodged visiting traders and stood surety for them. Market officials under the emir kept order and collected dues. Ordinary households bought daily in smaller neighbourhood markets, where women who could not go out sent children to sell for them.",
