    topics: [{ workplace: "household" }, { goal: "household" }, { aim: "household-home" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Kulle: secluded wives who still traded",
    text: "After the Sokoto jihad of 1804, wife seclusion, kulle, spread as a mark of respectability in Hausa towns. A married woman of a reputable household stayed inside the compound behind the zaure, the entrance hut where men received visitors. She was not idle. From her room she cooked snack foods, spun cotton, processed groundnuts into oil and cake, and sent children out to sell them, keeping the money as her own and investing it in her daughters' dowries of pots and cloth. A husband was bound to provide grain and housing; what a wife earned was hers. Poorer households could not afford seclusion, since their women had to farm and fetch water.",
