    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Kola nuts and sadaki: a Sahelian Muslim marriage",
    text: "In the Muslim Sahel, marriages were arranged between families, often with a cousin, and opened with gifts of kola nuts carried by a go-between to the girl's parents; accepting the kola meant accepting the suit. The groom owed the bride a sadaki, the Islamic dower, which was hers, and also gifts to her kin. A first marriage for a girl often came in her mid-teens, while men married later and might take up to four wives if they could provide for each. The bride's mother and female kin assembled her kayan daki, the room things: enamel bowls, pots and cloth, displayed and counted by neighbours. Divorce was common and remarriage expected.",
