    title: "Burial by nightfall and a widow's iddah",
    text: "Muslim practice in the Sahel buried the dead quickly, on the day of death if possible. The body was washed by people of the same sex, wrapped in white cloth, prayed over, and laid in the grave on its right side facing Mecca, without a coffin. Mourners gave alms, and the family held gatherings of Qur'an reading and sadaka on the third, seventh and fortieth days. A widow entered the iddah, a mourning seclusion of four months and ten days, wearing plain clothes and staying at home so that any pregnancy would be known. After it she could remarry, often a brother of her husband in some communities, though Islamic law did not require it. Graves were plain and rarely visited.",
