    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Yam heaps outside the Yoruba town",
    text: "Yoruba farmers lived in large walled towns and walked out, sometimes for hours, to farms in the surrounding bush, staying in farm huts, aba, in the busy season. The staple was yam, planted in heaps raised with a short iron hoe in the dry months, staked for its vines and harvested from July into the dry season; maize, cassava after the 1800s, okra, peppers and melon were interplanted. Land belonged to lineages and was allotted to members by the head of the compound; it could be lent but not sold until the colonial era. Men cleared and hoed. Women helped with planting and harvest but earned their living mainly by processing and trading the crops.",
    wiki: ["Yoruba people", "Dioscorea rotundata", "Shifting cultivation"],
