    text: "Yoruba markets were women's worlds. Every town had a daily market near the king's palace and periodic markets on a four- or eight-day cycle between towns, and women walked between them with goods on their heads: yams, palm oil, cloth, pots, cooked food. Each trade had its association, led by a senior woman, and the whole market by the iyaloja, the mother of the market, who settled disputes, fixed some prices and represented market women to the oba. A married woman kept her own trading income, owed her husband no account of it, and used it to feed her children and build standing in her own right. Credit came through ajo and esusu savings groups.",
