    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Aso oke on two kinds of loom",
    text: "Yoruba weaving was done on two looms by two sexes. Men wove on a narrow horizontal treadle loom, producing strips about ten centimetres wide that were sewn edge to edge into a cloth; this was aso oke, 'top cloth', used for wrappers, agbada robes and the head ties worn at ceremonies. Women wove wider panels on an upright single-heddle loom set against the house wall. Cotton was grown locally and spun by women with a drop spindle; indigo and local silk from wild moths, sanyan, gave colour and prestige. Iseyin and Oyo were famous weaving towns. Boys learned by sitting beside a father or master for years, beginning by winding bobbins.",
