    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Adire: starch-resist indigo of Abeokuta",
    text: "Adire, 'tie and dye', was women's craft. Dyers bought plain cloth, from the later nineteenth century mostly imported Manchester shirting, and resisted parts of it before dyeing: by tying, by stitching with raffia, or by painting on a paste of cassava starch with a feather or, in Abeokuta and Ibadan from about 1910, through zinc stencils cut by men. The cloth went into large pots of indigo fermented with wood ash, over and over. Patterns had names, such as Olokun, the sea goddess, and ibadandun, 'Ibadan is sweet'. Dyeing was organised in family compounds where girls learned from mothers and co-wives, and the dyers' associations defended their trade against cheaper synthetic dyes in the 1930s.",
