    title: "The agbo ile: a lineage under one roof",
    text: "A Yoruba household lived in an agbo ile, a large rectangular compound of rooms facing an inner courtyard, walled in mud and roofed with thatch or, later, corrugated iron. The men of a patrilineage lived there with their wives and children, each wife with her own room, and the eldest man, the bale, presided. Status came from age: juniors greeted seniors by kneeling or prostrating, and old parents were fed and served by sons' wives and grandchildren. Walls needed replastering and roofs rethatching after the rains, work for young men. The compound itself was a lineage's inheritance, not divided among heirs; people buried their dead beneath the floors of its rooms.",
