    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Esusu savings and iwofa pawning",
    text: "A Yoruba household short of money had two main resources. One was esusu or ajo, a rotating fund in which each member paid a fixed sum at every meeting and took the whole pot in turn; it paid for a wedding, a funeral or a trader's stock without interest. The other was iwofa, pawning: a debtor placed himself or a child with the creditor to work on his farm until the loan was repaid, the labour counting as the interest, not the principal. Pawns were not slaves and kept their family ties, but a debt could run for years. The colonial government outlawed pawning in the 1920s and 1930s; esusu continues today.",
