    title: "The alarina and the bride's ifa",
    text: "Marriages joined two lineages, not only two people. An intermediary, the alarina, carried messages between the families, and the bride's family often consulted an Ifa diviner before agreeing. The groom's family paid bridewealth, owo ori, in cash, cloth, kola and drink, and the groom might work on his future in-laws' farms for years before the wedding. On the wedding night the bride was led in procession to her husband's compound, singing ekun iyawo, the bride's lament for leaving her mother. She joined the compound as a junior 'wife' to everyone born there, even children, a status that improved as she bore children and grew older. Christian and Muslim weddings added their rites to these from the nineteenth century.",
