    title: "Babalawo, Ifa and the herbalist's leaves",
    text: "A troubled Yoruba household consulted a babalawo, 'father of secrets', a priest of Ifa who cast a chain of eight half-seeds, the opele, or manipulated sixteen palm nuts, and marked the resulting signs in wood dust on a carved tray. Each of the 256 signs, odu, called up a body of verses that the diviner had memorised over a long apprenticeship, and the verses prescribed a sacrifice. Healing was the work of the onisegun, the herbalist, who knew leaves, barks and incantations and treated fever, childbirth, snakebite and madness. Both were learned crafts, passed to sons and apprentices, and both were paid in money, cloth and animals.",
