    title: "Glass beads and brass heads of Ife",
    text: "Between about 1000 and 1400, Ile-Ife produced glass itself, not only reworked imports, a rare thing in West Africa. At the site of Igbo Olokun, workers melted local sand and lime with high alumina content into blue and green glass and drew it into beads, fired in crucibles whose fragments still litter the ground. The beads were royal regalia and traded far into the West African interior. In the same centuries Ife's metal casters made naturalistic heads in copper alloys by the lost-wax process, and its potters paved courtyards with potsherds set on edge. These crafts clustered around the palace and were probably attached to it, though the details of their organisation are not recorded.",
