    text: "In Benin City craftsmen were organised in hereditary guilds attached to the palace, each living in its own ward. The brass casters, Igun Eronmwon, lived on the street still named for them and worked only for the Oba: they cast commemorative heads for royal ancestral altars and the relief plaques that once covered the palace pillars, using brass manilla bracelets imported by the Portuguese as metal. Ivory carvers, Igbesanmwan, and wood carvers formed another guild; there were guilds for weavers, leatherworkers and blacksmiths. A guild member learned inside his family. Making certain objects without royal leave was forbidden. British troops looted thousands of these works in 1897.",
