    title: "Palace societies of the Benin kingdom",
    text: "Government in Benin ran through the Oba's palace. Freeborn men of Benin City could enter one of three palace associations, the Iwebo, who kept the regalia, the Iweguae, the Oba's personal attendants, and the Ibiwe, who looked after his wives and children, and rise through its grades by paying fees and serving well. Town chiefs, the Eghaevbo n'Ore, and the hereditary Uzama, kingmakers, balanced them. Villages paid tribute in yams, palm oil and labour through chiefs who held them as fiefs. The Oba himself was sacred and rarely seen, and the calendar was marked by royal rituals such as Igue, when his head, the seat of his fortune, was honoured.",
