    title: "Yam, the king of crops, and the Igbo yam barn",
    text: "For the Igbo the yam was a man's crop and a man's measure. Men cleared the bush in the dry season, raised mounds with a hoe and planted seed yams in February and March; women planted cocoyams, cassava and vegetables between the mounds and did most of the weeding. After harvest the yams were tied in rows to frames of upright poles in the ọba ji, the yam barn, where they could be counted by visitors and kept dry until the next planting. No new yam could be eaten until the priests had held the New Yam festival, Iri ji, in August or September, giving the first yams to Ahiajoku and the ancestors.",
