    text: "The Igbo week had four days, Eke, Orie, Afọ and Nkwọ, and each village held its market on one of them, so that a trader could visit a different market each day within walking distance. Markets were both commerce and politics: men met there to hear news and settle disputes, and women traded palm oil, cassava, cloth and pots. Women's associations, notably the umuada, daughters of the lineage married elsewhere, and the wives' groups, could fine and sanction anyone who disrupted a market. Long-distance trade ran through the Aro, who used the prestige of their oracle, Ibini Ukpabi, to travel safely, and paid for goods in brass manillas and cowries.",
