    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Second burial and the widow's year",
    text: "An Igbo death was handled in two stages. The body was buried soon, often in the compound, but the true funeral, ikwa ozu, came later, sometimes years later, when the family could afford the cattle, cloth, gunfire and masquerades it required; only then did the dead reach the land of the ancestors, rather than wander. The eldest son inherited the ofo staff that linked the family to its dead and poured libations to them. A widow shaved her head, wore dark cloth and stayed in mourning for a year, under strict rules watched by the husband's kinswomen, and could be taken as a wife by a brother of the dead man.",
