    title: "Awka smiths and the bronzes of Igbo-Ukwu",
    text: "Around the ninth century CE, casters at Igbo-Ukwu made intricate leaded-bronze vessels, shells and regalia by the lost-wax method, buried with a high-ranking man with tens of thousands of glass beads: evidence of skill and trade long before the forest kingdoms. In later centuries the smiths of Awka travelled all over Igboland, forging hoes, machetes, gongs and ceremonial staffs, and working as healers and agents for the Agbala oracle. They moved in groups for months, protected by their ritual connections, and set up forges in host villages. A son learned at his father's forge, working the bellows first. Iron tools were part of the price of a bride and a sign of standing.",
