    title: "Sitting on a man: the Women's War of 1929",
    text: "Igbo women had an old sanction against a man who abused his wife or broke market rules: they gathered at his compound, sang insulting songs, danced, and banged on his hut, sometimes pulling off the roof, until he apologised. It was called 'sitting on a man'. In November 1929, when rumours spread that British-appointed warrant chiefs would tax women, news passed from market to market, and tens of thousands of women sat on the chiefs and colonial offices across Owerri and Calabar provinces. Troops fired on the crowds, killing more than fifty. The British called it the Aba Riots; Igbo remember it as the Women's War, Ogu Umunwaanyi.",
