    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Akan gold: shafts, pans and brass weights",
    text: "Gold was mined in the Akan forest by farming families in the dry season. Men sank shafts up to twenty or thirty metres into the reef or the old stream beds, shoring them with timber, and hauled up ore in baskets; women and children crushed it on stones and washed it in wooden pans at the stream. Nuggets belonged to the chief or, in the Asante period, to the Asantehene, and dust to the miners. Gold dust was the currency: every trader carried scales, a spoon and a set of cast brass weights shaped as animals, proverbs and geometric forms, and weighing gold for a purchase was a skill a boy learned early.",
