    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Kente at Bonwire",
    text: "Kente was woven by men on a narrow double-heddle loom in villages near Kumasi, Bonwire the most famous. Strips about ten centimetres wide, woven in blocks of warp and weft patterning, were sewn together into a large wrapper worn toga-fashion by men and in two pieces by women. Early kente used cotton and silk unravelled from imported cloth; from the twentieth century, rayon. Each pattern had a name, and some were reserved for the Asantehene, who had the right to new designs and to wear them first. A weaver's son learned by winding thread and weaving plain strips before he was allowed a named pattern. Women were barred from the loom by a taboo.",
    wiki: ["Kente cloth", "Bonwire"],
