    topics: [{ aim: "household-home" }, { aim: "widowed-household" }, { aim: "raise-kin" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Abusua: belonging through the mother",
    text: "Akan people belonged to their mother's lineage, the abusua, and inherited from it. A man's heir was not his son but his sister's son, and his own children were members of their mother's line; a father gave a child its spirit, ntoro, and his name, but not land or office. Husband and wife often lived in separate houses of their own lineages, with the wife sending cooked food across the town at night. When a man died his property and his stool went to his abusua, and his widow and children could find themselves with little; this became a great source of complaint after cocoa made men rich, and missionaries pressed for wills leaving property to wives and children.",
