    title: "Adinkra cloth and the long Akan funeral",
    text: "An Akan funeral was the largest event in a family's life and was paid for by the abusua and by contributions from every mourner, recorded publicly. The body lay in state for mourners to wail over; then burial, with gifts for the journey to the ancestors. The funeral celebration followed on the eighth day and the fortieth, and again at the anniversary. Mourners wore dark red, black or russet cloth, some of it stamped with adinkra symbols in a dye of boiled bark, using calabash stamps cut into designs like Gye Nyame, 'except God'. Widows fasted, kept vigil and wore mourning for months; failing to honour a dead parent properly was a shame that lasted.",
