    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Kola north to Salaga",
    text: "Kola nuts grew in the Asante forest and were chewed across the Muslim Sahel, where they were a permitted stimulant, a gift of hospitality and a token at weddings. Asante's traders carried them in baskets lined with wet leaves, which had to be turned and rewetted every few days to stop the nuts spoiling, north to the great market at Salaga. There Hausa caravans from Kano bought them for cloth, leather, shea butter, natron and slaves. The state controlled the trade, taxed it and in the nineteenth century forbade northern merchants to come south of Salaga. Porters carried loads of about thirty kilograms for weeks.",
