    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Coral stone houses and the inner room",
    text: "Wealthy Swahili families lived in houses of coral stone set in lime mortar, with mangrove-pole ceilings and carved wooden doors. Rooms ran back from the street in a sequence of long galleries, each more private than the last, ending in the ndani, the innermost room where the women of the house lived and where brides were secluded before marriage and the dead were washed. Wall niches displayed imported porcelain. Houses passed through daughters as well as sons, and a father often built a room or house for a daughter at her marriage, so women held property. Commoners and slaves lived in houses of mud and palm-thatch, makuti, in the outer town.",
