    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Clove plantations of Zanzibar and Pemba",
    text: "After Sultan Seyyid Said moved his court from Oman to Zanzibar, fully by 1840, Omani landowners planted cloves across Zanzibar and Pemba, and within twenty years the islands supplied most of the world's cloves. The work was done by enslaved people brought from the mainland: they cleared land, planted and weeded, and at harvest climbed trees to pick the unopened buds, which were spread on mats to dry. Slaves had customary rights to some days on their own plots. The Zanzibar slave market closed in 1873 under British pressure, but slavery itself was abolished only in 1897, and many freed people stayed on as squatters and labourers on the same estates.",
