    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Nyamwezi porters on the central caravan road",
    text: "In the nineteenth century caravans of hundreds of porters walked the central road from Lake Tanganyika and Unyamwezi to the coast at Bagamoyo, carrying ivory, and returned with cloth, beads, brass wire and guns. Many porters, wapagazi, were Nyamwezi men who hired themselves out for the season and took pride in the work: they carried loads of about thirty kilograms of tusks or bales on the shoulder, marched in single file behind a guide with a flag, and sang as they went. A round trip might take a year. Caravan culture spread Swahili, Islam, new foods and diseases inland. Leaders like Mirambo and Tippu Tip built states and fortunes on it.",
    wiki: ["Nyamwezi people", "Tippu Tip", "Mirambo"],
