    title: "The kipande and the settler farm",
    text: "From 1920 every African man in Kenya over sixteen had to carry the kipande, a registration certificate in a metal box on a string round his neck, recording his fingerprints and employment; leaving an employer without discharge was a crime. Hut and poll taxes payable in cash pushed men into work on European farms in the 'White Highlands', from which Africans were barred as owners. Kikuyu families moved onto settler land as squatters, cultivating a plot and grazing stock in return for months of labour a year, until settlers cut their rights in the 1930s and 1940s. The grievances over land and the kipande fed the Kenya African Union and Mau Mau.",
    wiki: ["Kipande", "White Highlands", "Mau Mau rebellion"],
