    text: "At the confluence of the Limpopo and Shashe, by the early thirteenth century, a ruler lived on top of Mapungubwe hill while commoners lived below, the first clear separation of an elite from its people in southern Africa. The rulers' wealth came from ivory and gold sent down the Limpopo to the Swahili coast and exchanged for glass beads and cloth; tens of thousands of beads have been found. Graves on the hill held gold-foil objects, among them the famous little gold rhinoceros, made by hammering sheet gold over a wooden core. Commoners farmed sorghum and millet on the floodplain and herded cattle. A drier climate around 1300 ended the kingdom, and power shifted north to Great Zimbabwe.",
