    text: "Great Zimbabwe's walls, the Great Enclosure's up to eleven metres high, were built without mortar from granite blocks. Builders split the stone from granite domes that shed natural sheets, helped by fire and water to open the cracks, then dressed the blocks roughly to shape and laid them in even courses, with decorative chevron and herringbone patterns near the top. The walls enclosed and screened the homes of the ruler and his family and marked status; commoners lived in daga (mud) houses outside. The labour must have come from tribute owed to the ruler by a population of several thousand, probably working in the dry season after harvest.",
