    title: "Shona gold workings and the Mutapa state",
    text: "Thousands of ancient gold workings dot the Zimbabwe plateau. Shona miners followed gold-bearing quartz reefs down with narrow shafts and stopes, sometimes thirty metres deep, breaking the rock by fire-setting and hammering, and carrying it up in bark baskets. Women and children crushed and panned the ore. Mining was seasonal work between harvest and planting and paused when the water table rose. Rulers of Great Zimbabwe and later the Mutapa state took a share and controlled the trade, and Portuguese traders from Sena and Tete, whom the Mutapa kept at fairs outside his capital, bought gold with cloth. Colonial prospectors in the 1890s found their mines by following the 'ancient workings'.",
