    text: "A Shona burial was followed, about a year later, by kurova guva, 'beating the grave', the ceremony that brought the dead person's spirit home to become a protective ancestor, mudzimu. Relatives brewed beer, visited the grave, danced and sang through the night, and returned with the spirit to the homestead. Until then the spirit was unsettled and could cause harm. At this time the estate was settled and the widow chose whether to be inherited by a brother of her husband, usually by symbolic gesture: handing him water, or a bowl of beer, or refusing and staying single. A successor took the dead man's name and role.",
