    title: "The cattle byre at the centre",
    text: "An Nguni homestead, umuzi, was a circle of beehive-shaped grass houses facing inward on a central cattle byre, isibaya. The byre was men's space: cattle were penned there at night, the head of the homestead was buried there, ancestors were addressed there, and grain was stored in pits dug in its floor and sealed with dung, where the cattle's trampling kept it safe and cool. Women were not allowed in. Boys herded from childhood, driving cattle out after the morning milking and bringing them back at dusk, and learned every animal by its colour and horn shape; Zulu has dozens of words for cattle colour patterns. Milk, soured in calabashes, amasi, was a staple.",
