    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Ilobolo: bridewealth in cattle",
    text: "Among the Zulu, Xhosa, Swazi and Sotho, a marriage was made by the transfer of cattle from the groom's family to the bride's, ilobolo or bohali. Negotiators from each side met and bargained, often over months, and the cattle might be paid over years. The cattle did not buy the woman; they moved her children into her husband's lineage and compensated her family, who used them in turn to pay bridewealth for her brothers. If the marriage failed, cattle could be returned. From the nineteenth century, colonial authorities and Shepstone in Natal fixed limits on the number, and migrant wages increasingly paid lobola in cash, allowing young men to marry without their fathers' herds.",
