    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Isangoma and inyanga",
    text: "Nguni healing had two specialists. The isangoma, most often a woman, was called by the ancestors through illness and dreams, trained for months or years under an experienced diviner, and then divined the causes of misfortune, by trance, by throwing bones, or by asking the ancestors through whistling spirits. The inyanga, most often a man, was a herbalist who knew hundreds of plants, barks and animal parts and sold medicines for illness, protection and love; knowledge passed from father to son or by paid apprenticeship. Under colonial law in Natal, herbalists were licensed while diviners were suppressed, and both flourished in the townships.",
