    text: "Under Shaka and his successors in the early nineteenth century, young Zulu men of the same age were formed into regiments, amabutho, and served the king at royal homesteads for years, herding the king's cattle, working his fields, building and fighting. They could not marry until the king gave their regiment permission, usually in their thirties, and then took wives from a matching women's age group. Each regiment had its own name and shield colours. The system concentrated manpower, cattle and loyalty in the king. After the Anglo-Zulu war of 1879 the British broke it up, and young men's labour went instead to the mines and the farms.",
