    text: "Basotho boys became men through lebollo, an initiation school held in a secluded lodge in the mountains, lasting several months over winter. Initiates were circumcised, hardened by cold and discipline, taught the laws, histories and praise poetry of their chiefs, and on emerging composed their own praises, lithoko. Girls had their own school, where they were taught by older women and covered in white clay. Initiation made a young person marriageable and a full member of the chiefdom. Missionaries from the Paris Evangelical Mission, active in Lesotho from 1833, condemned it and many Christian families avoided it, but it persisted strongly.",
