    text: "The Basotho of the Caledon valley and the Lesotho lowlands grew sorghum and, from the nineteenth century, maize and wheat, which they sold in quantity to the Orange Free State and Kimberley markets in the 1870s. They adopted the ox-plough from missionaries and Boer neighbours in the 1860s, and men took over ploughing while women still hoed and weeded. Winters on the high ground are cold and snowy, and the Basotho blanket, wool traded from British mills from the 1860s, became their dress. Loss of the fertile lands west of the Caledon to the Free State in the 1860s wars, and soil erosion, eventually pushed men to the mines.",
