    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Mafisa: borrowing cattle",
    text: "In southern Africa, cattle wealth moved through loans. Among the Tswana a rich man placed cattle with poorer men under mafisa: the borrower herded them, drank their milk and could keep some of the calves, while the owner kept the herd and gained a dependent and a political follower. The Nguni had similar customs. A family ruined by drought, raids or cattle disease could rebuild a herd this way, and a young man could start one. Loans were remembered for generations and could be recalled, so a man's obligations tied him to his patron's fortunes. Rinderpest in 1896–97 killed most of the region's cattle and destroyed many such networks.",
