    text: "Gold was found on the Witwatersrand in 1886, and within years Johannesburg's mines were working the deepest shafts in the world. Black miners, recruited from Mozambique, Lesotho, Nyasaland and the reserves by the Witwatersrand Native Labour Association, signed contracts of nine to eighteen months, lived in closed compounds of concrete bunks, and did the hardest work: drilling with hand hammers, and later rock drills, in narrow stopes kilometres down, where silicosis, rockfalls and heat killed thousands. White miners held skilled jobs under a legal colour bar. Pay was low and fixed across the industry. In 1946 more than 70,000 African miners struck for higher wages and were forced back by police.",
