    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Kimberley's Big Hole and the closed compound",
    text: "Diamonds were found near Kimberley in 1869–71, and thousands of diggers, black and white, worked small claims in a single volcanic pipe that became the Big Hole. Pedi, Sotho, Tsonga and other men walked hundreds of kilometres to work for a few months and buy guns and cattle. As Cecil Rhodes's De Beers consolidated the claims in the 1880s, it moved African workers into closed compounds, fenced and roofed with wire, where they lived for the whole contract and were searched, even purged, before release to stop diamond theft. This model of the closed compound spread to the gold mines and shaped South African labour for a century.",
