    text: "By the 1930s, in Lesotho, southern Mozambique, the Transkei and much of Nyasaland, most young and middle-aged men were away at the mines or on farms for months or years at a time. Their wives ran the homestead alone: they ploughed or hired ploughing, herded with the children, built and mended houses, and waited for remittances, which bought cattle, paid lobola, taxes and school fees, and were often late or never came. Men who died underground left widows with little compensation. Wives' own mobility was controlled by chiefs and husbands, but some left for towns and brewed beer. Songs of the period, like Sotho women's lifela, describe the long absence.",
