    text: "Mission schools in southern Africa produced a class of African teachers, clerks, ministers, nurses and journalists. The Glasgow Missionary Society's Lovedale Institution in the eastern Cape, founded in 1841, taught Latin, printing, carpentry and theology and printed books in Xhosa; the South African Native College at Fort Hare opened beside it in 1916 and educated a generation of leaders. Parents paid fees by selling stock or saving migrants' wages. Converts, amakholwa, lived in mission villages and dressed and farmed in European style, and were sometimes looked down on by 'red' traditionalists. A mission-trained son could expect a salaried life, but after 1910 segregation law closed most higher posts to him.",
