    title: "Terraced rice and the angady",
    text: "Madagascar's first settlers, arriving from Borneo across the Indian Ocean in the first millennium CE, brought Austronesian rice farming, which merged with African cattle-keeping. In the central highlands, Merina and Betsileo farmers cut terraces into valley slopes and flooded them from canals. Men dug with the angady, a long-handled iron spade, and trampled the mud with herds of zebu before transplanting; women transplanted the seedlings from nurseries and harvested with knives. On the east coast, farmers practised tavy, slash-and-burn hill rice. Rice was eaten at every meal, and a meal without it was not counted a meal. Irrigation works around Antananarivo were organised by kings as fanompoana.",
    wiki: ["Agriculture in Madagascar", "Merina people", "Betsileo people"],
