    title: "Zebu, wealth and the tomb",
    text: "In western and southern Madagascar, among the Sakalava, Bara and Mahafaly, zebu cattle were the measure of a man. Young men herded them across dry grasslands, and cattle raiding, among the Bara, was a proof of manhood before marriage. Cattle were rarely eaten except at sacrifices, funerals and circumcisions, when many were slaughtered and their horns set on the tomb; a Mahafaly tomb could be crowned with dozens of horns and carved wooden posts, aloalo, showing the dead man's life. The number of cattle killed at a funeral displayed a family's standing, and wealth moved through cattle to the dead as much as to the living.",
