    text: "Malagasy people said that a house is for a lifetime but a tomb is forever, and families spent more on tombs than on their homes. The ancestors, razana, were the source of blessing and the keepers of custom, fady, which varied by place and family. In the highlands, families gathered every few years for famadihana, the turning of the bones: they opened the tomb, carried out the dead in their shrouds, wrapped them in new silk lamba, and danced with them before laying them back. Being buried away from the ancestral tomb was a great misfortune, so the bodies of those who died far away were brought home, sometimes years later.",
